<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-matrix-of-transformation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Matrix of a Linear Transformation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A linear transformation has no single matrix: it has one matrix per choice of basis
      for the domain and codomain. Changing those choices conjugates the matrix by a
      change of basis matrix, and a well-chosen basis can make the matrix strikingly
      simple.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-matrix-wrt-bases">
    <title>Four Matrices for One Transformation</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-four-matrices">
      <title>The Same Transformation in Different Bases</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> be the linear transformation given by
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} -5x + 6y \\ -3x + 4y \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          and let
          <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ \vec{e}_1, \vec{e}_2 \}
          = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
          and
          <m>\mathcal{B}' = \{ \vec{b}_1, \vec{b}_2 \}
          = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
          be the bases.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="I">
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_1</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_2</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}'</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_3</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}</m> and
                <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_4</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> and
                <m>\mathcal{B}</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First note the relations between the two bases, which we will need throughout:
          <md>
            \vec{e}_1 = -\vec{b}_1 + \vec{b}_2,
            \qquad
            \vec{e}_2 = 2\vec{b}_1 - \vec{b}_2,
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Since
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \\ -3 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          and
          <m>T \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 6 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> We compute the images of the new basis vectors and express
          them in the new basis:
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \quad \text{and} \quad
            \left[ \begin{matrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          <md>
            T \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \quad \text{and} \quad
            \left[ \begin{matrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which gives
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Alternatively, starting from
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m> and inserting
          <m>P^{-1}P = I</m>,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1 \,
            \underbrace{P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}^{-1}
            P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}}_{I} [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}}
            = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}},
          </md>
          i.e., <m>A_2 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}'} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}'}</m> where
          <md>
            A_2 = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1 \,
                  P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}^{-1}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
          Note how much simpler <m>A_2</m> is than <m>A_1</m>: in the basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> the transformation just scales <m>\vec{b}_1</m> by <m>1</m>
          and <m>\vec{b}_2</m> by <m>-2</m>. Indeed
          <md>
            T \left( x\vec{b}_1 + y\vec{b}_2 \right)
            = x \, T(\vec{b}_1) + y \, T(\vec{b}_2)
            = x\vec{b}_1 - 2y\vec{b}_2.
          </md>
          This is exactly the phenomenon that
          <xref ref="ch-eigenvalues" text="type-global" /> is about.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III) First method.</term> Express the images of the <m>\mathcal{B}</m>
          basis vectors in the <m>\mathcal{B}'</m> basis:
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{e}_1) \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \left[ \begin{matrix} -5 \\ -3 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = -1 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} - 2 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \; \longrightarrow \; \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{e}_2) \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = \left[ \begin{matrix} 6 \\ 4 \end{matrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}'}
            = 2 \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + 2 \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            \; \longrightarrow \; \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          so
          <m>A_3 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III) Second method.</term> From
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}}</m>, multiply both
          sides by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}}</m>:
          <md>
            A_3 = P_{\mathcal{B}' \leftarrow \mathcal{B}} \, A_1
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV) First method.</term>
          <md>
            \left[ T(\vec{b}_1) \right]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \left[ T(\vec{b}_2) \right]_{\mathcal{B}} = \begin{bmatrix} -4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad \Longrightarrow \qquad
            A_4 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV) Second method.</term>
          <md>
            A_4 = A_1 \, P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'}
                = \begin{bmatrix} -5 \amp 6 \\ -3 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          or equally
          <md>
            A_4 = P_{\mathcal{B} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}'} \, A_2
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -4 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-four-matrices-p2">
      <title>The Same Game in <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>T : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{P}_2</m> be the linear transformation
          <md>
            T\left( p(x) \right) = p(x) + p'(x),
          </md>
          and consider the ordered bases
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ 1, \; 1 + x, \; 1 + x + x^2 \}</m> of
          <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="I">
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_1</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute the matrix <m>A_2</m> of <m>T</m> with respect to <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_3</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
                and <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the matrix <m>A_4</m> of <m>T</m> if the bases <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>
                and <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> are used for domain and codomain respectively.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Unlike the previous example, the vectors here are polynomials, not columns.
          The coordinate maps are the bridge: they are isomorphisms
          <m>\mathbb{P}_2 \to \R^3</m>, and once every polynomial is replaced by its
          coordinate vector, all four computations become the same matrix arithmetic in
          <m>\R^3</m> as before.
        </p>
        <p>
          First the change-of-basis matrices, exactly as in
          <xref ref="eg-p2-change-of-basis" text="type-global" />: reading the elements
          of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> columnwise, and
          inverting,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Transform the basis elements of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> and read
          the outputs in <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>:
          <md>
            T(1) = 1, \qquad
            T(x) = x + 1, \qquad
            T(x^2) = x^2 + 2x,
          </md>
          whose coordinate vectors form the columns
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II) First method.</term> Transform the basis elements of
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> and express the outputs in <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              T(1) \amp = 1 = 1(1) \; \longrightarrow \; (1, 0, 0),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T(1 + x) \amp = 2 + x = 1(1) + 1(1 + x) \; \longrightarrow \; (1, 1, 0),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T(1 + x + x^2) \amp = 2 + 3x + x^2
              = -1(1) + 2(1 + x) + 1(1 + x + x^2) \; \longrightarrow \; (-1, 2, 1),
            </mrow>
          </md>
          so
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II) Second method.</term> Exactly as in the previous example,
          <md>
            A_2 = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1 \,
                  P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(III)</term> Multiplying
          <m>A_1 [\, p \,]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = [\, T(p) \,]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m> on the
          left by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> converts the
          output coordinates,
          <md>
            A_3 = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a spot check on the second column: <m>T(x) = 1 + x</m> is itself the second
          element of <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>, so its <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinates are
          <m>(0, 1, 0)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(IV)</term> This time the input coordinates arrive in
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> and must be converted before <m>A_1</m> can act:
          <md>
            A_4 = A_1 \, P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                  \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a spot check on the third column:
          <m>T(1 + x + x^2) = 2 + 3x + x^2</m>, whose
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>-coordinates are <m>(2, 3, 1)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-polar-frame-matrix">
      <title>The Matrix between Cartesian and Polar Frames</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="eg-cartesian-polar" text="type-global" /> we attached to each
          angle <m>\theta</m> the rotated orthonormal basis
          <md>
            \mathcal{B}_{\theta} = \{ \vec{u}_r, \vec{u}_\theta \}
            = \left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta \\ \sin\theta \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} -\sin\theta \\ \cos\theta \end{bmatrix}
            \right\}
          </md>
          of <m>\R^2</m>. Compute the matrix that converts between Cartesian
          coordinates and <m>\mathcal{B}_{\theta}</m>-coordinates, and use it for
          <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \sqrt{3} \end{bmatrix}</m> with
          <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The conversion is the matrix of the identity transformation
          <m>\operatorname{id} : \R^2 \to \R^2</m> with respect to the basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}_{\theta}</m> of the domain and the standard basis
          <m>\mathcal{E}</m> of the codomain. Its columns are the basis vectors of
          <m>\mathcal{B}_{\theta}</m> written in Cartesian coordinates, which is
          exactly the rotation matrix by the angle <m>\theta</m>:
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{E} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_{\theta}}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta \amp -\sin\theta \\ \sin\theta \amp \cos\theta \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Because the columns are orthonormal, the inverse is simply the transpose
          <mdash /> rotation by <m>-\theta</m>:
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_{\theta} \leftarrow \mathcal{E}}
            = \left( P_{\mathcal{E} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_{\theta}} \right)^{-1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta \amp \sin\theta \\ -\sin\theta \amp \cos\theta \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{3}</m>,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{E} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_{\pi/3}}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1/2 \amp -\sqrt{3}/2 \\ \sqrt{3}/2 \amp 1/2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          and the two coordinate vectors of <m>\vec{v}</m> convert into each other as
          promised:
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{E} \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_{\pi/3}}
            \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \sqrt{3} \end{bmatrix}
            = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{E}},
            \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_{\pi/3} \leftarrow \mathcal{E}}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \sqrt{3} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1/2 + 3/2 \\ -\sqrt{3}/2 + \sqrt{3}/2 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
            = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}_{\pi/3}}. \checkmark
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          A word of caution. This matrix converts between two systems of
          <em>basis</em> coordinates <mdash /> Cartesian on one side, the polar frame
          pinned at the angle <m>\theta</m> on the other, where the first
          <m>\mathcal{B}_{\theta}</m>-coordinate of a radial vector is the polar
          <m>r</m>. No matrix can send
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \end{bmatrix}</m> to the polar pair
          <m>(r, \theta)</m> itself: as we saw in
          <xref ref="eg-cartesian-polar" text="type-global" />, that map is not linear.
          The change between Cartesian and polar <em>frames</em> is linear; the change
          between Cartesian and polar <em>coordinates</em> is not.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-transformation-between-spaces">
    <title>A Transformation between Different Vector Spaces</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-p2-to-m22">
      <title>From <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> to <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> be the space of all <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices and
          let <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> be the space of all polynomials of at most degree
          <m>2</m>. Define <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m> as the
          transformation given by
          <md>
            S \left( a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0 \right)
            = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          for all <m>a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0 \in \mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Describe the null space of <m>S</m>. Is <m>S</m> one-to-one?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Express the range of <m>S</m> as a span of a linearly independent set of
                <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices. Is <m>S</m> onto?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Is <m>S</m> an isomorphism?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Extend the basis of the range of <m>S</m> such that the extended basis,
                <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>, spans <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and the
                ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and find the
                matrix of the transformation <m>S</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> and the
                ordered basis
                <md>
                  \mathcal{B}_2 = \left\{
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
                    \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
                </md>
                for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and find the matrix of the transformation
                <m>S</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term>
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} S = \left\{ P(x) = a_2 x^2 + a_1 x + a_0
            \; : \; S(P(x)) = 0_{2 \times 2} \right\}.
          </md>
          Setting
          <m>S(P(x)) = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> gives
          <md>
            a_1 + a_2 = 0, \quad a_2 + a_0 = 0, \quad a_1 - a_0 = 0, \quad a_1 + a_0 = 0,
          </md>
          and the last two force <m>a_1 = a_0 = 0</m>, whence <m>a_2 = 0</m>. This is the
          trivial solution.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hence the null space only contains the zero vector of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>, which
          is known as the zero polynomial
          <m>P_0(x) = (0)x^2 + 0(x) + 0(1) = 0</m>, and <m>S</m> is one-to-one:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} S = \{ P_0(x) \}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> The output of <m>S</m> is
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}
            = a_0 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_0}
            + a_1 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_1}
            + a_2 \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}}_{M_2}.
          </md>
          We can check that <m>\{ M_0, M_1, M_2 \}</m> is a linearly independent set and
          hence this set is a basis for the range of <m>S</m>, since the range of <m>S</m>
          is <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ M_0, M_1, M_2 \}</m>. This means
          <m>\dim (\text{range of } S) = 3</m>, whereas
          <m>\dim (\mathbb{M}_{22}) = 4</m>. Therefore <m>S</m> is NOT onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> No. <m>S</m> is not onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(4)</term> We want to add a fourth matrix, <m>M_3</m>, such that
          <m>M_3</m> cannot be written as
          <m>M_3 = a_0 M_0 + a_1 M_1 + a_2 M_2</m>. In other words, writing
          <m>M_3 = \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \amp b_2 \\ b_3 \amp b_4 \end{bmatrix}</m>, the
          following must NOT have a solution:
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} b_1 \amp b_2 \\ b_3 \amp b_4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} a_1 + a_2 \amp a_2 + a_0 \\ a_1 - a_0 \amp a_1 + a_0 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a system of linear equations in <m>a_2, a_1, a_0</m> we have
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp b_2 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp b_3 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_4
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow \cdots \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|c}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b_1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b_4 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp b_3 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp b_3 + b_2 - b_1
            \end{array} \right].
          </md>
          If <m>b_3 + b_2 - b_1 \neq 0</m>, then the system is inconsistent. For example,
          choose <m>b_1 = 1</m>, <m>b_2 = b_3 = b_4 = 0</m>, which gives
          <m>M_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore, the set
          <md>
            \mathcal{B}_1 = \{ M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3 \} = \left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
          </md>
          is a basis for <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(5)</term> The strategy is captured by the following diagram. The
          coordinate maps identify <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> with <m>\R^3</m> and
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> with <m>\R^4</m>, so the transformation <m>S</m>
          corresponds to a matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-commutative-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The transformation <m>S : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{M}_{22}</m>
            and the matrix transformation <m>\R^3 \to \R^4</m> induced by the
            coordinate isomorphisms.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-commutative-diagram-S" width="62%">
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="commutative-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(460,280)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,10,6]">
                  <group at="commutative-square">
                    <label at="node-P2" anchor="(1.5,5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></label>
                    <label at="node-M22" anchor="(8.5,5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R3" anchor="(1.5,1)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{R}^3</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R4" anchor="(8.5,1)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\mathbb{R}^4</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-S" endpoints="[(2.4,5),(7.3,5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-S" anchor="(4.85,5.3)" alignment="north" scale="1.4"><m>S</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-left-iso" endpoints="[(1.5,4.3),(1.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>
                    <line at="arrow-right-iso" endpoints="[(8.5,4.3),(8.5,1.7)]" arrows="2"/>

                    <line at="arrow-A" endpoints="[(2.2,1),(7.6,1)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-A" anchor="(4.9,0.35)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\text{matrix transformation}</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="commutative-square"
                              text="A commutative square relating the transformation S to a matrix transformation">
                    <annotation ref="arrow-S" text="Across the top, S maps the polynomial space P 2 to the space of 2 by 2 matrices M 2 2"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-left-iso" text="On the left, the coordinate isomorphism identifies P 2 with R 3 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-right-iso" text="On the right, the coordinate isomorphism identifies M 2 2 with R 4 in both directions"/>
                    <annotation ref="arrow-A" text="Across the bottom, a matrix transformation maps R 3 to R 4"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          At the level of the ordered bases, <m>S</m> sends the basis
          <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m> of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> to the four matrices
          <m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m> spanning <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>, and the induced
          matrix transformation is given by a matrix <m>A</m>:
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-basis-diagram-S">
          <caption>
            The action of <m>S</m> on the ordered basis and the corresponding
            matrix <m>A</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-basis-diagram-S" width="92%">
            <shortdescription>A commutative square in which S carries the ordered basis of P2 to four matrices, while the matrix A carries the standard basis columns of R3 to those of R4.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A square diagram. Across the top, <m>S</m> sends the ordered basis
                <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m> of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> to the four matrices
                <m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m> spanning <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Down each side a double-headed arrow marks a coordinate isomorphism: on
                the left it identifies <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m> with the three standard basis
                columns of <m>\R^3</m>, and on the right it identifies
                <m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m> with the four standard basis columns of
                <m>\R^4</m>.
              </p>
              <p>
                Across the bottom, the matrix <m>A</m> carries the standard basis columns
                of <m>\R^3</m> to those of <m>\R^4</m>.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="basis-diagram-S">
              <diagram dimensions="(700,360)" margins="6">
                <coordinates bbox="[0,0,12.6,7.6]">
                  <group at="basis-square">
                    <label at="node-B" anchor="(2.3,6.5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-B1" anchor="(9.7,6.5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\{M_0, M_1, M_2, M_3\}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R3" anchor="(2.3,1.5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m></label>
                    <label at="node-R4" anchor="(9.7,1.5)" alignment="center" scale="1.4"><m>\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-S" endpoints="[(3.6,6.5),(7.4,6.5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-S" anchor="(5.5,6.85)" alignment="north" scale="1.4"><m>S</m></label>

                    <line at="arrow-left" endpoints="[(2.3,5.8),(2.3,3.05)]" arrows="2"/>
                    <line at="arrow-right" endpoints="[(9.7,5.8),(9.7,3.2)]" arrows="2"/>

                    <line at="arrow-A" endpoints="[(4.5,1.5),(7.0,1.5)]" arrows="1"/>
                    <label at="label-A" anchor="(5.75,1.85)" alignment="north" scale="1.4"><m>A</m></label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Here is how the elements of <m>\mathcal{B}</m> are transformed:
          <md>
            S(1) = M_0, \qquad S(x) = M_1, \qquad S(x^2) = M_2,
          </md>
          which means, reading each image in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>,
          <md>
            A_1 = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}_{4 \times 3}.
          </md>
          To find <m>A_1</m> we had to express <m>S(1)</m>, <m>S(x)</m>, <m>S(x^2)</m> in
          terms of the basis elements <m>M_0, M_1, M_2</m>, and <m>M_3</m>. In other
          words, we needed to figure out how the basis elements of the domain transform
          with respect to the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> of the codomain.
        </p>
        <p>
          After finding the output of the matrix transformation, we can use the
          isomorphism between <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> and <m>\R^4</m> and write the output
          as a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(6)</term> Write
          <m>M_{12} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>M_{21} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>M_{22} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
          <m>M_{11} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>, so that
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2 = \{ M_{12}, M_{21}, M_{22}, M_{11} \}</m>. Then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              S(1) \amp = M_0 = M_{12} - M_{21} + M_{22},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              S(x) \amp = M_1 = M_{21} + M_{22} + M_{11},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              S(x^2) \amp = M_2 = M_{12} + M_{11},
            </mrow>
          </md>
          and therefore
          <md>
            A_2 = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Alternatively, we can find a matrix that changes the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
          to <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>. From
          <md>
            M_0 = M_{12} - M_{21} + M_{22}, \quad
            M_1 = M_{21} + M_{22} + M_{11}, \quad
            M_2 = M_{12} + M_{11}, \quad
            M_3 = M_{11},
          </md>
          we get
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              -1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{and} \qquad
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} \, A_1 = A_2.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The reason is the familiar one: from
          <m>A_1 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}_1}</m>, multiplying both
          sides by <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> gives
          <m>A_2 [\vec{u}]_{\mathcal{B}} = [\vec{v}]_{\mathcal{B}_2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-trace-transformation">
      <title>The Trace as a Transformation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The <term>trace</term> of a square matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries.
          On <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m> it defines the map
          <m>\operatorname{tr} : \mathbb{M}_{22} \to \R</m>,
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}
            = a + d,
          </md>
          where the codomain <m>\R</m> is itself a vector space, of dimension <m>1</m>,
          with ordered basis <m>\{ 1 \}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Show that <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> is a linear transformation.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Describe <m>\operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})</m> and find a basis
                for it. Is <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> one-to-one?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What is the range of <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>? Is it onto?</p></li>
            <li><p>Is <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> an isomorphism?</p></li>
            <li><p>Verify the rank<ndash />nullity relation for <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Consider the ordered basis
                <m>\mathcal{B} = \{ M_{11}, M_{12}, M_{21}, M_{22} \}</m> of
                <m>\mathbb{M}_{22}</m>, where <m>M_{ij}</m> has a <m>1</m> in position
                <m>(i,j)</m> and zeros elsewhere, and the ordered basis <m>\{1\}</m> of
                <m>\R</m>. Find the matrix of the transformation
                <m>\operatorname{tr}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use that matrix to compute
                <m>\operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 7 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix}</m>
                through coordinates.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the matrix of <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> if the basis of the
                codomain is changed to <m>\{ 2 \}</m>?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term> For matrices
          <m>M = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <m>N = \begin{bmatrix} a' \amp b' \\ c' \amp d' \end{bmatrix}</m> and a scalar
          <m>k</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \operatorname{tr}(M + N) \amp = (a + a') + (d + d')
              = \operatorname{tr} M + \operatorname{tr} N,
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \operatorname{tr}(k M) \amp = ka + kd = k \operatorname{tr} M,
            </mrow>
          </md>
          so <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> respects addition and scalar multiplication and is
          a linear transformation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> A matrix is in the null space exactly when its diagonal
          entries cancel:
          <md>
            \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})
            = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp -a \end{bmatrix}
              \; : \; a, b, c \in \R \right\}
            = \operatorname{Span} \left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}.
          </md>
          The three spanning matrices are linearly independent (they occupy different
          entries), so they form a basis and
          <m>\dim \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr}) = 3</m>. Since the null space
          contains nonzero vectors <mdash /> for instance
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} \mapsto 0</m> <mdash />
          the transformation is NOT one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> Every real number is a trace: for any <m>r \in \R</m>,
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr} \begin{bmatrix} r \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} = r.
          </md>
          Hence the range is all of <m>\R</m> and <m>\operatorname{tr}</m> is onto.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(4)</term> No. An isomorphism must be one-to-one and onto, and by (2)
          the trace is not one-to-one. (No transformation
          <m>\mathbb{M}_{22} \to \R</m> could be an isomorphism: the dimensions
          <m>4</m> and <m>1</m> do not match.)
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(5)</term> From (2) and (3),
          <md>
            \underbrace{\dim (\text{range of } \operatorname{tr})}_{1}
            + \underbrace{\dim \operatorname{Nul} (\operatorname{tr})}_{3}
            = 4 = \dim (\mathbb{M}_{22}),
          </md>
          as the rank<ndash />nullity relation predicts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(6)</term> We transform the basis elements of the domain and read each
          output in the basis <m>\{1\}</m> of <m>\R</m>:
          <md>
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{11}) = 1, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{12}) = 0, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{21}) = 0, \quad
            \operatorname{tr}(M_{22}) = 1.
          </md>
          Each column of the matrix is the coordinate vector of one output, which here is
          a single number, so the matrix of the transformation is the
          <m>1 \times 4</m> row
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The corresponding matrix transformation <m>\R^4 \to \R</m> tells the same
          story as (2) and (3) in the language of pivots: <m>A</m> has a pivot in its
          only row, so it is onto, but it cannot have a pivot in every one of its four
          columns, so it is not one-to-one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(7)</term> In the basis <m>\mathcal{B}</m>,
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp -3 \\ 7 \amp -9 \end{bmatrix} \right]_{\mathcal{B}}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \\ 7 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            A \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \\ 7 \\ -9 \end{bmatrix}
            = 4 - 9 = -5,
          </md>
          which agrees with computing the trace directly: <m>4 + (-9) = -5</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(8)</term> With respect to the basis <m>\{2\}</m> of <m>\R</m>, a real
          number <m>r</m> has coordinate <m>r/2</m>, since <m>r = \tfrac{r}{2}(2)</m>.
          Each entry of the matrix is now the <m>\{2\}</m>-coordinate of the
          corresponding output:
          <md>
            A' = \begin{bmatrix} \tfrac{1}{2} \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp \tfrac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As in the previous example, changing a basis of the codomain rescales the rows
          of the matrix without changing the transformation itself.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-p2-change-of-basis">
      <title>A Change of Basis in <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the standard ordered basis
          <m>\mathcal{B}_1 = \{ 1, x, x^2 \}</m> of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> and the set
          <md>
            \mathcal{B}_2 = \{ 1, \; 1 + x, \; 1 + x + x^2 \}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Show that <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is a basis of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the change-of-basis matrix
                <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m>, which converts
                <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>-coordinates into <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>-coordinates.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the coordinates of <m>q(x) = 3 + 2x + 4x^2</m> relative to the
                basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(1)</term> Since <m>\dim \mathbb{P}_2 = 3</m>, it is enough to show
          that the three polynomials are linearly independent. Suppose
          <md>
            a(1) + b(1 + x) + c(1 + x + x^2) = 0,
          </md>
          that is,
          <md>
            (a + b + c) + (b + c)x + c x^2 = 0.
          </md>
          Comparing coefficients gives <m>c = 0</m>, then <m>b + c = 0</m> forces
          <m>b = 0</m>, and then <m>a + b + c = 0</m> forces <m>a = 0</m>. Only the
          trivial combination produces the zero polynomial, so <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is
          linearly independent and hence a basis of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(2)</term> The easy direction is the other one: each element of
          <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m> is already written in terms of <m>\{1, x, x^2\}</m>, so
          reading off coordinates columnwise,
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          The matrix we want is its inverse,
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
          = \left( P_{\mathcal{B}_1 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_2} \right)^{-1}</m>, which we
          can also compute directly by expressing each element of <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m>
          in the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>:
          <md>
            1 = (1), \qquad
            x = (1 + x) - (1), \qquad
            x^2 = (1 + x + x^2) - (1 + x),
          </md>
          so that
          <md>
            P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          In the language of this section,
          <m>P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1}</m> is the matrix of the
          identity transformation
          <m>\operatorname{id} : \mathbb{P}_2 \to \mathbb{P}_2</m> with respect to the
          basis <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> of the domain and the basis <m>\mathcal{B}_2</m>
          of the codomain: the transformation does nothing, and the matrix only
          translates between the two coordinate systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(3)</term> Relative to <m>\mathcal{B}_1</m> the coordinates of
          <m>q</m> are read off directly, and the matrix converts them:
          <md>
            [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_1} = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_2}
            = P_{\mathcal{B}_2 \leftarrow \mathcal{B}_1} [q(x)]_{\mathcal{B}_1}
            = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          As a check,
          <md>
            1(1) - 2(1 + x) + 4(1 + x + x^2)
            = (1 - 2 + 4) + (-2 + 4)x + 4x^2
            = 3 + 2x + 4x^2 = q(x).
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
